%\VignetteIndexEntry{CellChat: Rust-accelerated inference}
%\VignetteEngine{knitr::knitr}
%\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\title{CellChat: Rust-accelerated inference}
\author{cellchat-rs authors}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{What this package is}

\texttt{CellChat} re-implements CellChat's numeric inference kernel in Rust and exports
the matching inference entry points with the same object structure. Code using the unqualified
API can use the accelerator by loading \texttt{CellChat} in place of the original CellChat. Visualization
and the Shiny app stay upstream R code. What follows uses only
\texttt{CellChat}; parity against pinned upstream CellChat
(commit \texttt{75253cd}, v2.2.0.9001) is established by the repository's test suite
(427 differential comparisons, a 200-configuration matrix, metamorphic and fuzz suites --
see \texttt{tests/parity/}), not re-proved here.

<<setup, include=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
library(CellChat)
@

\section{A complete run on synthetic data}

A tiny synthetic dataset -- 300 cells, 3 groups, 60 genes, 4 ligand-receptor pairs --
exercises the whole accelerated path in seconds. Real-data timings are quoted in
Section~\ref{sec:speedup} from pre-measured, protocol-bound runs, not from this chunk.

<<synthetic>>=
set.seed(11)
nC <- 300L
grp <- factor(rep(c("A", "B", "C"), each = 100))
## Four real ligand-receptor pairs from the bundled database export, so the vignette exercises
## genuine complex/cofactor resolution rather than toy names that match nothing. A synthetic pair
## table (e.g. G1/G2) would resolve against no export: the kernel reads complexes from the pinned
## CellChatDB export, and invented names make it raise "subscript out of bounds" for a missing
## subunit -- which is the correct behaviour for names outside the database, and the wrong thing
## for a vignette to demonstrate. Single-gene ligands and receptors with empty cofactor fields keep
## the demo small while staying inside the real schema.
dbix <- system.file("db", "human", "interactions.tsv", package = "CellChat")
dblines <- strsplit(readLines(dbix), "\t", fixed = TRUE)
is_simple <- vapply(dblines, function(f) {
  ## Single genes only: complex names join subunits with underscores (`ACVR1_TGFBR1`), and the
  ## `[,;]` test alone lets those through. A non-empty annotation is required too: pairs without
  ## one have no pathway, and `computeCommunProbPathway` groups by pathway -- an empty grouping
  ## variable fails downstream in `tapply()` with "arguments must have same length", which names
  ## neither the pair nor the missing annotation.
  nzchar(f[3]) && nzchar(f[4]) && !grepl("[,;_]", f[3]) && !grepl("[,;_]", f[4]) &&
    !nzchar(f[5]) && !nzchar(f[6]) && !nzchar(f[7]) && !nzchar(f[8]) && nzchar(f[9])
}, TRUE)
pick <- Filter(function(f) f[[1]] != "", lapply(which(is_simple)[1:4], function(i) dblines[[i]]))
genes <- paste0("G", seq_len(60))
sig_genes <- unique(unlist(lapply(pick, function(f) c(f[3], f[4]))))
genes[seq_along(sig_genes)] <- sig_genes
X <- matrix(rpois(60L * nC, lambda = 2), nrow = 60,
            dimnames = list(genes, paste0("c", seq_len(nC))))
LRsig <- data.frame(
  interaction_name = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 1),
  pathway_name = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 2),
  ligand = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 3),
  receptor = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 4),
  agonist = "", antagonist = "",
  co_A_receptor = "", co_I_receptor = "",
  annotation = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 9),
  row.names = vapply(pick, `[[`, "", 1),
  stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
DB <- list(complex = data.frame(),
           cofactor = data.frame(),
           interaction = LRsig,
           geneInfo = data.frame(Symbol = genes))
meta <- data.frame(labels = grp, row.names = colnames(X))
cellchat <- createCellChat(object = log1p(X), meta = meta, group.by = "labels")
cellchat@DB <- DB
cellchat <- subsetData(cellchat)
## Thresholds wide open, and stated as such: synthetic noise has no truly
## over-expressed genes, so the tutorial defaults would keep zero pairs and the rest of
## this vignette would run on an empty network. \texttt{thresh.p = 1} keeps every tested
## gene; on real data (Section~\ref{sec:speedup}) the defaults apply.
cellchat <- identifyOverExpressedGenes(cellchat, do.fast = FALSE, thresh.p = 1)
cellchat <- identifyOverExpressedInteractions(cellchat)
cellchat <- computeCommunProb(cellchat, type = "triMean", nboot = 10L, seed.use = 1L)
cellchat <- filterCommunication(cellchat, min.cells = 5)
cellchat <- computeCommunProbPathway(cellchat)
cellchat <- aggregateNet(cellchat)
dim(cellchat@net$prob)
@

The object has the same shape, slots, dimnames and option fields an upstream run
produces; downstream code (\texttt{subsetCommunication}, plotting, export) is
unaffected because nothing about the object changes except how fast it was computed.

\section{Measured speedup}
\label{sec:speedup}

The numbers below are pre-measured on the pinned host (8c/16t AMD Zen 2, 32\,GB),
taskset-pinned, medians over $\geq 5$ timed repeats with bootstrap 95\% CIs, and
reproducible via \texttt{bench-runner/bench\_real.R} and
\texttt{bench-runner/bench\_amdahl.R}. They are quoted here so the vignette states
what was measured rather than re-measuring something smaller and incomparable.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\hline
dataset & upstream & CellChat & speedup & Amdahl limit \\
\hline
human skin (7,563 cells, kernel, nboot=100) & 101.4\,s & 2.41\,s & 42.1x & -- \\
human skin (full pipeline) & 100.1\,s & 6.0\,s & 16.7x & 24.8x \\
mouse wound (21,557 cells, kernel, nboot=100) & 245.3\,s & 9.41\,s & 26.1x & -- \\
mouse wound (full pipeline) & 202.3\,s & 20.9\,s & 9.7x & 13.3x \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two readings, stated in the order that matters. The \emph{pipeline} number (9.7--16.7x)
is what a user running the vignette workflow observes: the kernel is 92--96\% of
upstream's pipeline, and the remaining $\sim$10\,s is I/O, preprocessing and
over-expression analysis that no kernel accelerates (all $\sim$1x, as it should be --
both sides run the same presto-backed code there). The \emph{kernel} number (22--42x)
is the gated, bit-identical computation in isolation. Quoting only the second would imply
more than the evidence supports; the Amdahl limit column states the ceiling implied by
the measured shares.

Bit-identity holds on the authors' own data, not just on fixtures: 1,207,952
\texttt{Prob} values across both datasets with maximum absolute difference exactly
zero, and a log-log parity scatter in \texttt{bench-runner/results/}.

\section{Scope and honest limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item Four centrality measures (\texttt{hub}, \texttt{authority}, \texttt{eigen},
\texttt{page\_rank}) call igraph directly: they are iterative solvers whose output
varies run to run, so reimplementing them would trade identical-by-construction for a
reimplementation that breaks with igraph's next tolerance retune. Degrees, strengths
and betweenness are computed in Rust, bit-identical to igraph.
\item Spatial neighbour search uses an exact k-d tree where upstream uses approximate
Annoy. On the authors' visium data the two differ in 42 of 64 inter-group distances
(2.8\% max) and 19.6\% of \texttt{Prob} entries (1.0\% of peak) -- a systematic bias
in the approximation, not noise (Annoy agrees with itself). See
\texttt{bench-runner/results/spatial\_divergence.json}.
\item \texttt{identifyOverExpressedGenes(do.fast=TRUE)} delegates to presto: a different
algorithm (normal approximation), not a faster Wilcoxon. Both branches select the same
features on the fixtures; p-values agree to 3.8e-3 with no threshold crossing.
\item Upstream's recommendation to downsample large datasets is a runtime workaround.
With the I/O floor measured in seconds, subsetting cells to fit a runtime budget is
unnecessary -- the correction is quantitative (the Amdahl table above), not rhetorical.
\end{itemize}

\end{document}
